{
	title: 'Web push: notifications on a phone with the web app on its home screen',
	spec: 'The web client is an installable web app and can get push notifications, including on an iPhone that added it to its home screen (iOS 16.4+; Safari tabs get no push, only home-screen apps do). Pieces, stdlib and WebCrypto only: the host serves a manifest (name Hal, display standalone, icons) and a service worker (/sw.js) whose push handler shows the notification and whose notificationclick opens or focuses the session\'s page. A "Notify me" control in the web client (shown only when the Push API exists and permission is not yet granted; the permission prompt must come from that tap, never on load) subscribes with the host\'s VAPID public key and sends the subscription to the host over the WebSocket. The host keeps a P-256 VAPID key pair in state/ (made once, 0600) and subscriptions per browser in state/, drops one when the push service answers 404 or 410, and sends a push (RFC 8030 POST to the subscription endpoint, VAPID JWT ES256 per RFC 8292, payload encrypted aes128gcm per RFC 8291, TTL a few hours) when a session needs the user: a turn completed or failed, a question or approval waits. It skips the push when some client has that session focused and visible (clients report visibility), so a user at the screen is not buzzed. The payload is small: session name or id and one line (e.g. "done", "asks: …"), never tool output or secrets. The app icon badge counts sessions needing attention (navigator.setAppBadge) where supported. Pushes need HTTPS, so they work through the webUrl deployment (HTTPS reverse proxy), not http://localhost. A setting push (default true) turns sending off. Tests: VAPID JWT verifies with the public key; payload encryption decrypts with a test subscription\'s keys (RFC 8291 test vector); a 410 drops the subscription; no push while a client has the session visible; a push after a completed turn when none has.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['8a']
}
